1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,953
Welcome to the detailed public tutorial for Spatial Methods Workbench, version zero point two point one.

2
00:00:07,953 --> 00:00:23,400
This video shows how to prepare analysis-ready spatial or imaging data, choose among twelve workflows, configure an analysis, run a synthetic example, and preserve results with provenance and citations.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:32,806
The Workbench combines built-in exploratory methods with direct adapters to the public ISPat, GP-GHS, and ISPat 3D engines.

4
00:00:32,806 --> 00:00:42,441
It reports numerical outputs and fitted objects, but it does not automatically assign biological, clinical, or causal meaning.

5
00:00:42,441 --> 00:00:49,171
Every value shown in the demonstration is synthetic and contains no patient information.

6
00:00:49,521 --> 00:01:08,365
By the end, you should be able to determine whether a dataset satisfies a workflow's input contract, distinguish the spatial scales represented by the network methods, configure an analysis without overlooking major controls, and preserve the run as a reproducible bundle.

7
00:01:08,365 --> 00:01:16,818
You will also see which jobs belong on the public demonstration service and which require a controlled local installation.

8
00:01:16,818 --> 00:01:19,035
This is an operational tutorial.

9
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:29,773
A separate, local interpretation course connects reported quantities to the respective publications and should be used together with subject-matter review.

10
00:01:30,123 --> 00:01:36,332
The Workbench deliberately separates computation from scientific interpretation.

11
00:01:36,332 --> 00:01:48,595
It checks structural compatibility, performs the selected calculation, and returns numerical tables, figures, fitted objects, software details, and citations.

12
00:01:48,595 --> 00:02:01,014
It does not convert an edge into a signaling claim, a cluster into a cell state, a coefficient into a clinical conclusion, or an indirect effect into causality.

13
00:02:01,014 --> 00:02:09,086
Those conclusions require study design, domain expertise, sensitivity analysis, and often collaboration.

14
00:02:09,086 --> 00:02:19,719
Only public, release-scoped material is included in this software.

15
00:02:20,069 --> 00:02:28,259
The common input is a CSV containing processed measurements and the variables required by the chosen method.

16
00:02:28,259 --> 00:02:37,587
Applicable units can include cells, spots, subcellular locations, pixels, voxels, subjects, contours, sections, or volumes.

17
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:58,594
Examples include coordinates paired with phenotypes or marker intensities from multiplex imaging, processed spatial transcriptomic or proteomic features, registered three-dimensional serial sections, aligned neuroimaging features, and tabular exposures, mediators, or outcomes.

18
00:02:58,594 --> 00:03:07,695
The method's statistical sampling unit must be explicit; a row is not automatically an independent biological replicate.

19
00:03:08,045 --> 00:03:20,366
The application does not segment raw microscopy, register serial sections, read raw DICOM, process whole-slide images, or transform raw sequencing reads.

20
00:03:20,366 --> 00:03:25,440
Complete those steps first and retain the preprocessing record.

21
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:43,238
Before uploading, document segmentation and phenotype rules, coordinate units and origin, registration quality, normalization and transformations, batch handling, missingness, exclusions, and the biological sampling unit.

22
00:03:43,238 --> 00:03:55,076
Confirm that feature columns are numeric where required and that repeated cells, spots, slides, or subjects are represented honestly in the design.

23
00:03:55,426 --> 00:03:59,303
Start with the quantity the study aims to estimate.

24
00:03:59,303 --> 00:04:05,385
Use Moran's I for global spatial autocorrelation under a defined neighbor graph.

25
00:04:05,385 --> 00:04:13,519
Use conditional-network methods for associations that remain after conditioning on other measured features.

26
00:04:13,519 --> 00:04:25,910
Use ISPat for shared and discrete zone-specific network structure, GP-GHS when edges may vary continuously across coordinates, and ISPat 3D for registered volumes.

27
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:41,494
Separate paths cover held-out prediction, exploratory spatial clustering, shallow neural prediction, wide scalar-image screening, image-to-image latent screening, mediation, and shape principal components.

28
00:04:41,494 --> 00:04:47,727
Method complexity should follow the question and design, not the other way around.

29
00:04:48,077 --> 00:04:55,212
Open the live application and load the synthetic demonstration or a non-sensitive CSV.

30
00:04:55,212 --> 00:05:08,733
In the mapping panel, assign x and y coordinates, optional z, zone, sample or volume identifier, outcome or class, exposure, mediator, and the numeric feature set.

31
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:26,486
The demonstration uses epithelial, antigen-presenting cell, cytotoxic T cell, regulatory T cell, and helper T cell labels to provide a biologically recognizable context, but the measurements are entirely simulated.

32
00:05:26,486 --> 00:05:38,349
On real data, compare every mapping with a data dictionary and check units, transformations, factor levels, identifiers, and repeated sampling.

33
00:05:38,699 --> 00:05:41,764
Open the Readiness tab after mapping.

34
00:05:41,764 --> 00:05:48,558
Each workflow is marked ready or blocked, with the missing roles or package named.

35
00:05:48,558 --> 00:05:56,097
Two-dimensional ISPat needs x, y, a zone, multiple numeric features, and its public engine.

36
00:05:56,097 --> 00:06:01,234
ISPat 3D additionally needs z and a volume or spot identifier.

37
00:06:01,234 --> 00:06:14,158
Imaging paths require aligned feature matrices, mediation requires exposure, mediator, and outcome roles, and classification requires a valid class outcome.

38
00:06:14,158 --> 00:06:19,046
A ready badge confirms only the minimum interface contract.

39
00:06:19,046 --> 00:06:23,686
It is not a power calculation or scientific endorsement.

40
00:06:24,036 --> 00:06:31,621
The first workflow asks whether nearby observations have similar values under a specified spatial graph.

41
00:06:31,621 --> 00:06:40,592
Select a numeric feature, choose the number of nearest neighbors, set the permutation count and seed, and run the analysis.

42
00:06:40,592 --> 00:06:49,126
The synthetic example uses a symmetrized eight-nearest-neighbor graph and one hundred ninety-nine label permutations.

43
00:06:49,126 --> 00:07:01,379
Its Moran statistic is approximately zero point seven eight two with a permutation p-value of zero point zero zero five because the pattern was intentionally generated.

44
00:07:01,379 --> 00:07:07,652
This tutorial records the calculation only; it does not assign a biological mechanism.

45
00:07:07,652 --> 00:07:16,842
Real studies should compare plausible graphs and use permutations that respect patients, slides, regions, or other dependence.

46
00:07:17,192 --> 00:07:32,335
The fast conditional-network path estimates a covariance matrix, adds a ridge penalty for stability, inverts it to obtain a precision matrix, and standardizes off-diagonal terms as partial correlations.

47
00:07:32,335 --> 00:07:37,658
It can produce an overall screen and exploratory zone-specific screens.

48
00:07:37,658 --> 00:07:43,056
Record the selected features, ridge penalty, threshold, and group sizes.

49
00:07:43,056 --> 00:07:51,078
The visible graph is a thresholded view of a continuous matrix, so preserve the full matrix and edge table.

50
00:07:51,078 --> 00:08:01,124
This screen does not perform the spatial adjustment or hierarchical Bayesian estimation of ISPat or GP-GHS and is labeled accordingly.

51
00:08:01,474 --> 00:08:05,869
ISPat is the dedicated two-dimensional spatial network engine.

52
00:08:05,869 --> 00:08:15,086
It is designed for spatially adjusted intensity surfaces together with shared and region-specific covariance or loading structure.

53
00:08:15,086 --> 00:08:25,933
A typical biological use is to compare conditional association patterns across prespecified tissue zones while retaining information shared across zones.

54
00:08:25,933 --> 00:08:37,347
The Workbench validates roles and passes the controls to the public ISPAT package; it does not substitute a simplified algorithm when that engine is unavailable.

55
00:08:37,347 --> 00:08:47,343
Preserve the continuous matrices, edge signs, threshold choices, zone definitions, sampler settings, convergence evidence, and fitted object.

56
00:08:47,343 --> 00:08:54,149
Network edges remain conditional associations rather than causal or physical-interaction claims.

57
00:08:54,499 --> 00:09:04,945
GP-GHS is appropriate when network relationships may change continuously across a tissue or spatial domain instead of only between discrete zones.

58
00:09:04,945 --> 00:09:13,245
The public method uses a spatial basis representation together with grouped horseshoe shrinkage over edge functions.

59
00:09:13,245 --> 00:09:23,477
The Workbench exposes engine controls and returns spatial edge summaries, selection information, and the fitted object supplied by the package.

60
00:09:23,477 --> 00:09:32,707
Record the basis construction, coordinate scaling, shrinkage and threshold settings, chain configuration, and convergence checks.

61
00:09:32,707 --> 00:09:41,436
The public tutorial identifies these outputs but leaves study-specific interpretation to the researcher and collaborators.

62
00:09:41,786 --> 00:09:44,652
ISPat 3D adds volumetric structure.

63
00:09:44,652 --> 00:09:55,381
It requires x, y, and z coordinates, a volume or spot identifier, a zone, multiple numeric features, and the public ISPAT3D engine.

64
00:09:55,381 --> 00:10:08,731
Its pipeline combines marker-specific three-dimensional spatial adjustment with multi-study factor analysis to estimate shared and zone-specific network structure.

65
00:10:08,731 --> 00:10:20,524
Registration is not performed inside the Workbench; serial sections or volumes must already share a scientifically defensible coordinate system.

66
00:10:20,524 --> 00:10:32,482
Record voxel or cell spacing, section order, registration procedures, zones, feature scales, factor settings, and the returned volumetric objects.

67
00:10:32,832 --> 00:10:45,063
The prediction path fits a linear model for continuous outcomes or logistic model for binary outcomes and reports held-out performance, predictions, and coefficients.

68
00:10:45,063 --> 00:10:52,800
Select predictors before examining the test set, document transformations, and verify the outcome coding.

69
00:10:52,800 --> 00:11:01,569
Random row splitting is often optimistic when nearby observations or records from the same patient appear in both sets.

70
00:11:01,569 --> 00:11:18,738
For confirmatory use, create patient-level, specimen-level, site-level, slide-level, or spatially blocked validation outside the convenience demonstration, and retain calibration and uncertainty alongside a single performance number.

71
00:11:19,088 --> 00:11:26,143
Spatial clustering augments standardized features with weighted coordinates and applies k-means.

72
00:11:26,143 --> 00:11:38,490
The coordinate weight explicitly changes the compromise between molecular similarity and spatial compactness, so explore stability across prespecified values and seeds.

73
00:11:38,490 --> 00:11:45,032
The output is an exploratory partition, not a validated cell state or tissue compartment.

74
00:11:45,032 --> 00:11:54,880
The shallow neural workflow provides a compact nonlinear predictor with controls for hidden units, weight decay, seed, and evaluation.

75
00:11:54,880 --> 00:12:02,670
It is an educational or screening path, not a claim that generic neural networks solve spatial dependence.

76
00:12:02,670 --> 00:12:07,154
Use grouped validation and compare against simpler baselines.

77
00:12:07,504 --> 00:12:17,507
The scalar-image ridge screen relates aligned high-dimensional image or voxel features to a scalar outcome using regularization.

78
00:12:17,507 --> 00:12:27,979
The image-to-image latent screen compresses an aligned source image, predicts latent target scores, and reconstructs the target image.

79
00:12:27,979 --> 00:12:34,231
Both return numerical coefficients or factors, predictions, and error summaries.

80
00:12:34,231 --> 00:12:40,483
Alignment, a common mask, scaling, and leakage-free preprocessing are essential.

81
00:12:40,483 --> 00:12:43,609
These are intentionally labeled screens.

82
00:12:43,609 --> 00:12:57,050
They do not claim to reproduce the full posterior selection, spatial priors, uncertainty, or varying-coefficient structure of the cited SV-NN, ST-CAR, or SBLF publications.

83
00:12:57,400 --> 00:13:08,185
The mediation workflow fits the component models and reports model-based indirect, direct, and total effects, with optional bootstrap uncertainty.

84
00:13:08,185 --> 00:13:21,925
Causal language requires temporal ordering, appropriate treatment of exposure, and strong assumptions about confounding and model form; the application does not certify those conditions.

85
00:13:21,925 --> 00:13:28,130
Shape PCA requires consistently defined and ordered landmark or contour coordinates.

86
00:13:28,130 --> 00:13:36,329
After centering and scaling, it summarizes geometric variation through principal component scores and loadings.

87
00:13:36,329 --> 00:13:44,381
Check correspondence, orientation, missing landmarks, and preprocessing before treating shapes as comparable.

88
00:13:44,731 --> 00:13:48,277
Before running, review every visible control.

89
00:13:48,277 --> 00:13:51,744
Neighborhood size defines the spatial graph.

90
00:13:51,744 --> 00:13:56,157
Permutation strategy defines the reference distribution.

91
00:13:56,157 --> 00:14:00,411
Penalties stabilize estimates but change coefficients.

92
00:14:00,411 --> 00:14:11,128
Display thresholds change only the rendered network if the continuous matrix is retained, whereas model thresholds may affect selection.

93
00:14:11,128 --> 00:14:14,752
Coordinate weights change clustering geometry.

94
00:14:14,752 --> 00:14:18,456
Hidden units and decay alter neural complexity.

95
00:14:18,456 --> 00:14:21,844
Components and factors control compression.

96
00:14:21,844 --> 00:14:27,596
Bayesian basis and sampler settings affect approximation and computation.

97
00:14:27,596 --> 00:14:39,652
Prespecify primary settings where possible, justify them scientifically, and plan sensitivity analyses rather than selecting a visually appealing result.

98
00:14:40,002 --> 00:14:44,519
The run button becomes available after citation acknowledgement.

99
00:14:44,519 --> 00:14:54,259
This is not a substitute for scholarly practice; it is a reminder that the interface and the underlying method are distinct contributions.

100
00:14:54,259 --> 00:15:04,070
Cite Spatial Methods Workbench using its versioned Zenodo DOI, and cite every method-specific publication listed for the selected workflow.

101
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:16,422
The downloaded citation file and manifest preserve the relevant entries, workflow identifier, software version, mapped variables, parameters, runtime, and session information.

102
00:15:16,422 --> 00:15:23,268
Review the final bibliography rather than copying citations without checking their applicability.

103
00:15:23,618 --> 00:15:29,982
After computation, audit the output rather than reading only the headline number.

104
00:15:29,982 --> 00:15:37,839
Confirm sample and feature counts, labels, reference levels, coordinate units, parameters, and seed.

105
00:15:37,839 --> 00:15:44,910
Inspect warnings and, for Bayesian engines, convergence diagnostics and sampling adequacy.

106
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:57,009
Preserve continuous network matrices behind thresholded graphs, prediction vectors behind performance summaries, and latent scores behind reconstructions.

107
00:15:57,009 --> 00:16:03,766
Verify that no preprocessing or validation step leaked information across test groups.

108
00:16:03,766 --> 00:16:16,809
The Workbench exposes reproducibility artifacts, but researchers remain responsible for assessing whether the analysis is scientifically and statistically defensible.

109
00:16:17,159 --> 00:16:21,356
Download the ZIP bundle from the Reproducibility tab.

110
00:16:21,356 --> 00:16:33,471
It contains the result table, figure, machine-readable JSON manifest, complete R result object, software and method citations, and collaboration contact.

111
00:16:33,471 --> 00:16:43,369
The manifest records the workflow, mappings, selected features, controls, runtime, summaries, package version, and R session.

112
00:16:43,369 --> 00:16:48,041
The input dataset is not duplicated into the result bundle.

113
00:16:48,041 --> 00:17:00,078
Under study governance, store the bundle with a checksum of the frozen analysis input, data dictionary, preprocessing log, protocol, and any deviations.

114
00:17:00,078 --> 00:17:07,205
This creates a traceable run without placing sensitive source data in the public artifact.

115
00:17:07,555 --> 00:17:14,045
For real data, clone the public repository and follow the complete installation guide.

116
00:17:14,045 --> 00:17:24,082
Install the core interface dependencies, run all thirty-one automated checks, and launch with shiny run App from the repository root.

117
00:17:24,082 --> 00:17:40,157
The included Docker configuration can help pin the environment, but production use still needs authentication, resource limits, logging, job management, backups, retention rules, and institutional security review.

118
00:17:40,157 --> 00:17:50,799
Use a controlled local or institutional deployment for protected information, long computations, or analyses governed by data-use agreements.

119
00:17:50,799 --> 00:17:56,685
Never upload protected health information to the public demonstration service.

120
00:17:57,035 --> 00:18:03,808
Install ISPAT, GP-GHS, and ISPAT 3D from the public repositories named in the guide.

121
00:18:03,808 --> 00:18:11,872
ISPAT relies on Stan, so compilers, package compilation, and the first run can take additional time.

122
00:18:11,872 --> 00:18:21,870
Confirm exact package versions and run a small synthetic smoke test through each Workbench adapter before a formal analysis.

123
00:18:21,870 --> 00:18:27,998
Archive installation logs, session info, adapter output, and fitted objects.

124
00:18:27,998 --> 00:18:37,674
For Bayesian jobs, plan memory, chains, warmup, sampling iterations, convergence review, and job persistence explicitly.

125
00:18:37,674 --> 00:18:43,157
The public host is not designed as a durable high-performance queue.

126
00:18:43,507 --> 00:18:48,601
A defensible study record extends beyond the application download.

127
00:18:48,601 --> 00:19:04,731
Freeze and checksum the analysis data, retain the data dictionary and preprocessing provenance, prespecify the primary estimand and controls, and define how dependence is handled in permutations or validation.

128
00:19:04,731 --> 00:19:17,233
Evaluate sensitivity to scientifically plausible neighbor graphs, thresholds, penalties, factor counts, spatial basis choices, or sampler settings as appropriate.

129
00:19:17,233 --> 00:19:27,034
Keep diagnostics and complete fitted objects, document deviations, and seek independent or external replication where possible.

130
00:19:27,034 --> 00:19:33,980
These steps are what connect a reproducible computation to a credible scientific analysis.

131
00:19:34,330 --> 00:19:42,954
Spatial Methods Workbench is open-source under the MIT License and archived as version zero point two point one on Zenodo.

132
00:19:42,954 --> 00:19:57,303
Use the live app for synthetic demonstrations and moderate non-sensitive jobs, the website guide for complete installation and restrictions, and the repository documentation for function-level contracts.

133
00:19:57,303 --> 00:20:08,895
Cite DOI ten point five two eight one slash Zenodo dot two one seven six four one nine six together with the underlying method publications recorded in each bundle.

134
00:20:08,895 --> 00:20:19,357
For method selection, study-specific interpretation, deployment planning, or collaborative analysis, contact Sagnik Bhadury through the application.

